text:
sam_g: Customer reports intermittent 401s when calling Redwood from a Cloudflare Worker (edge). Works fine from Node, fails from CF/Vercel edge. Using @redwood/inference-js v1.4.3. Repro snippet they gave:
```js
import { Redwood } from '@redwood/inference-js'
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: GLOBALS.REDWOOD_KEY })
export default async (req) => {
  const out = await client.generate({ model: 'redwood-alpha', prompt: 'hello' })
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(out))
}
```

meera_support: Can you paste the response headers from the failing call and confirm whether the request is coming directly from the browser -> our API, or browser -> worker -> our API? Also what exact SDK package+version?

sam_g: It's browser -> Cloudflare Worker -> Redwood API. SDK is v1.4.3 (browser bundle). Capture from the browser devtools shows an OPTIONS preflight then a blocked POST. Response headers from preflight: 
```
HTTP/1.1 204 No Content
access-control-allow-origin: https://app.customer.com
access-control-allow-methods: GET, POST, OPTIONS
# NOTICE: access-control-allow-headers is missing (so browser blocks)
```
Actual POST has Authorization: Bearer <token> header. If they change SDK to send x-api-key instead it goes through. Intermittent 401s seen when Worker forwards but sometimes our edge LB returns 401.

alex_runtime: Two quick things: 1) Authorization is a non-simple header so the browser requires the server to include it in Access-Control-Allow-Headers. If the CF worker (or any proxy) doesn't echo that ACAH, the browser will not send the actual POST. 2) Our hosted API intentionally disallows long-lived API keys from being embedded in public browsers; we return 401 for requests where origin is present and the key looks like a secret — this is a safety policy. That can look like "intermittent 401" depending on routing and which edge node is enforcing checks.

Suggested immediate debug steps:
- Capture request as received by our edge: can you share the raw request headers we received (I can look them up if you give me the customer org id + approximate ts)?
- Try a direct Worker -> Redwood fetch (no browser involved) with the same key to see if the Worker can reach us server-to-server. Example: in CF worker do a fetch(...) and return response to you. If that succeeds, the problem is browser preflight/CORS.
- For browser flows: either 1) do not put permanent keys in browser (recommended), or 2) ensure your Worker adds Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization (and ACAO/ACAM) on the preflight/response that the browser sees.

jordan_devex: Also note: older SDK browser bundles sometimes add Authorization in a way that makes the header show up as a different casing after some bundlers which confuses some proxies. Two action items for the team: 1) recommend customer upgrade to @redwood/inference-js v1.5.x (we added edge/runtime detection and clearer error for browser-key usage), 2) provide a cookbook for Edge runtimes (Vercel/CF) showing a secure pattern (proxy or short-lived token exchange).

Here is a short worker pattern we recommend (server-side auth in worker, return only sanitized data to browser):
```js
addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith(handle(event.request))
})
async function handle(req) {
  // server-side call using secret stored in worker KV/ENV
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + SECRET },
    body: req.body
  })
  const body = await r.text()
  // set CORS so browser can read the worker response
  return new Response(body, { headers: { 'access-control-allow-origin': 'https://app.customer.com' } })
}
```

meera_support: For the customer we should propose two options: A) short-term: switch Worker to set x-api-key header (works as a compatibility workaround) OR have the worker return proper ACAH including Authorization; B) long-term: issue them a short-lived client token (via our token-exchange endpoint) + update docs to call this out for any browser/edge usage. I'll offer both in the support reply.

sam_g: I'll ask for the org id + sample ts so Alex can pull the raw ingress logs. Customer is nervous about putting any secret in the browser so they prefer the token-exchange + proxy pattern.

ops-bot: deploy-bot: rolled out @redwood/inference-js v1.5.2 to npm (docs/recipes/edge-cookbook updated) :rocket:

alex_runtime: If you send me the log window I'll check whether our edge rejected because of policy or because the worker stripped/rewrote headers. Quick workaround for them until token-exchange is in place: run the worker as the server-side proxy (store secret in worker env) and make the browser talk only to the worker (worker must set ACAO/ACAH headers). :thumbsup:

jordan_devex: I'll add a short snippet to docs reminding that Vercel Edge uses the Edge Runtime and you should use the edge-compatible bundle or server-side proxy for secrets. Will link in the support reply. :white_check_mark:
